{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The 'integrates' relation is a valid substantive connection, but SRC_001 proposes that SRC_002 *integrates* SRC_001 — and the integration it describes is one of correction, not unification: per its own text, SRC_001's '-2 mod 5' observation is merely a 'partial truth' that SRC_004's 'algebraic correction' fixes (mod 7 needs -3). An integration that absorbs only the non-refuted fragment of a target, while explicitly replacing the target's attempted cross-modulus pattern with a different derivation, is better classified as a reduction or contradiction, not an integration. The stated kind does not match the stated content.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":false,"case":"SRC_002 correctly names a 'shares mechanism' relation: SRC_001 and SRC_003 both attempt the constant-difference shortcut (assuming one residue c mod lcm applies across moduli), and the unequal offsets (3-5=-2 vs 4-7=-3) provide a genuine, specific shared failure mode. The 'refuted if' test (that -2 and -3 would have to coincide modulo one of the bases) is coherent and non-tautological. No substantive fault in the relation kind or its content."},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's 'abstracts' relation is mislabeled as to kind: the described step — projecting the mod-5 residue 3≡-2 onto the mod-7 residue to get a single n≡-2 mod 35 — is an unwarranted generalization/overextension from one modulus to both, not an abstraction. The target's own 'refuted if' test exposes this: the failure is that 4≡-3 (not -2) mod 7, i.e., the derived claim does not hold for the source material it claims to abstract. An abstraction should preserve essential structure across instances; here the structure breaks exactly at the second modulus, so the failure is better described as a contradiction or false reduction, not abstraction.","counterexample":null}]}